---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';

/**
 * Careful here: lumper fee payment practices vary by facility and carrier.
 * Describe the recordkeeping problem; do not assert who is required to pay,
 * who reimburses, or how any of it is treated. No invented statistics about
 * how common cash payment is.
 */
---

<Article
  slug="scale-tickets-and-lumper-fees"
  toolLine="If cash payments and faded slips have been going unrecorded, the calculator gives you a sense of the scale."
>
  <p class="lede">
    Most receipts fail in one way. These two fail in opposite ways, which is
    what makes them the hardest records in the job to hold on to.
  </p>

  <p>
    A scale ticket usually exists and then becomes unreadable. A lumper payment
    is often readable if it exists at all &mdash; the problem is that it may
    never have existed. Different failures, different fixes.
  </p>

  <h2>Scale tickets: the record that erases itself</h2>

  <p>
    Weigh slips are thermal paper, which means they carry the same defect as
    fuel receipts: the printing is a chemical reaction in a coating, and heat,
    sunlight, oils, and friction all degrade it. A slip that lives in a cab
    through a summer is on a timer.
  </p>

  <p>
    What makes scale tickets worse than an ordinary fuel receipt is the moment
    they are handed over. You are moving, there is a queue, and the slip goes
    wherever your hand happens to put it &mdash; a visor, a door pocket, the
    dash. It is one of the least deliberate filing decisions in the whole day.
  </p>

  <p>
    Then it stays there. The visor above the windshield is roughly the hottest,
    most light-exposed surface in the vehicle, which makes it very close to the
    worst available storage location for thermal paper. And it is where these
    slips end up more often than anywhere else.
  </p>

  <p>
    The fix is unglamorous: photograph it before you pull away. Not at the end of
    the day, not at the end of the week &mdash; while it is in your hand and
    still says something.
  </p>

  <h2>Lumper fees: the record that may not exist</h2>

  <p>
    Lumper fees are a different problem entirely, and a harder one, because the
    failure happens before storage is even relevant.
  </p>

  <p>
    These payments happen at a dock, sometimes at an awkward hour, sometimes in
    cash, and the documentation practices vary enormously between facilities.
    Some produce a printed receipt without being asked. Some produce something
    handwritten. Some produce nothing at all unless you specifically ask for it
    &mdash; and at four in the morning with a load waiting, asking is exactly
    the thing that gets skipped.
  </p>

  <p>
    So the money leaves and no record is created. That is a different situation
    from a faded receipt: there is nothing to photograph, nothing to file, and
    nothing to reconstruct from later beyond your own memory of an amount.
  </p>

  <h3>What actually helps</h3>

  <p>
    Three things, in descending order of usefulness:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Ask for the receipt every time, before handing over money.</strong>
      This is the whole ballgame. Afterwards you have no leverage and no reason
      for anyone to help you.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Photograph whatever you do get, immediately.</strong> Handwritten
      slips are often on poor paper, in pen that smudges, and they are easy to
      lose because they do not look like official documents.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>If nothing is offered, make your own record at the time.</strong>
      A note of the date, the facility, the amount, and what it was for is
      substantially better than nothing, and it is far better made at the dock
      than reconstructed in April. Publication 463 stresses that adequate
      records are timely kept &mdash; made at or near the time. A
      contemporaneous note is a real record; a retrospective guess is not.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    None of this tells you how such a payment is treated on a return. That is a
    question for a tax professional, and it may depend on your arrangement with
    the carrier. What is entirely within your control is whether any record
    exists to discuss.
  </p>

  <h2>Cash is the common thread</h2>

  <p>
    Both of these sit near the cash end of the spectrum, and cash is where
    recordkeeping gets genuinely hard.
  </p>

  <p>
    A card payment leaves a trace whether or not you keep the receipt. The
    statement is not a complete record &mdash; it does not show what was bought
    &mdash; but it establishes that money moved, to whom, and when. That
    fallback is doing more work than most people realise.
  </p>

  <p>
    Cash has no fallback. If the slip is lost or was never issued, there is
    nothing. Publication 583 does list petty cash slips among supporting
    documents, which is a reminder that a simple contemporaneous record of a
    cash payment is a recognised form of documentation &mdash; but somebody has
    to actually create it.
  </p>

  <h2>The habit, for both</h2>

  <p>
    For scale tickets: photograph before pulling away, because the visor is
    where records go to die.
  </p>

  <p>
    For lumpers: ask first, photograph second, and if neither produces anything,
    write it down on the spot.
  </p>

  <p>
    Both take a few seconds at a moment when a few seconds feels like a lot. The
    alternative is a year with a hole in it that you cannot describe precisely
    enough to do anything about.
  </p>

  <p>
    For the wider list, see
    <a href="/blog/what-receipts-to-keep-trucking">what receipts to keep</a>. For
    what to do when a record is already gone, see
    <a href="/blog/what-happens-if-you-lose-a-receipt">what happens if you lose a receipt</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
